After Effects 2D Animation & Rigging Design Document
Purpose: A practical reference and agent demonstration guide for 2D character preparation, cutout rigging, and mesh-based deformation in Adobe After Effects—built around the core principle of separating rigid (locked) structure from flexible (editable) motion.
1. Template Philosophy: Rigid Core vs. Flexible Parameters
Just like a reusable design document separates locked structural rules from editable fields, 2D rigging in After Effects separates solid structural parts (anchored torsos, locked joints, rigid guidelines) from flexible, animated areas (rotating limbs, swaying antennae, secondary follow-through). Each demonstration module below specifies:


* Rigid / Locked (Do Not Alter): Core setup rules, hierarchy, and native After Effects tools that agents must follow instead of writing custom Python scripts.
* Flexible / Editable (Agent Demo Parameters): Timing, pin placement, bend amplitude, and visual styling that agents can adjust when demonstrating each concept.
2. Vector vs. Raster Asset Workflows
Selecting the correct asset pipeline determines whether artwork retains clarity under high scaling and how deformation tools behave on the rendering stack:


* Raster Workflow (Photoshop .psd): Pixel-based artwork ideal for painterly textures, shading, and photographic detail. Scaling a raster layer up significantly can cause pixelation (Adjust layer properties in After Effects), so prepare artwork at or above the largest display size needed in the composition (Preparing and importing still images). Save layered PSDs in RGB (or Grayscale) color mode with unlocked, uniquely named layers, and import via Composition - Retain Layer Sizes so each layer's anchor point centers on its own artwork rather than the full composition frame (Preparing and importing still images).
* Vector Workflow (Illustrator .ai / Shape Layers): Path-based artwork that scales cleanly when Continuously Rasterize is enabled in the Timeline Switches column (Managing layers in After Effects). Note two critical caveats from Adobe's documentation:
   1. Enabling Continuously Rasterize changes the layer render order to Masks → Transformations → Effects (Managing layers in After Effects).
   2. Do not animate Position or Scale on a continuously rasterized layer with layer transformations while also animating it with Puppet tools; instead, precompose the vector/shape layer first and apply Puppet tools to the precomposition (Animating with Puppet tools).


