	3. Asset Preparation & Inpainting for Clean Joints
Characters cannot be animated effectively as a single flat image without breaking physical realism during articulation:


* Layer Separation: Break the character into independent layers for every movable part (e.g., torso, head, upper arm, lower arm, hand, wings, antennae). In Illustrator, use Release To Layers before importing (Preparing and importing still images).
* Inpainting Hidden Joint Areas: When a limb rotates, it exposes the area underneath it. Artists use inpainting (AI fill or manual cloning/painting in Photoshop) to extend the torso behind the shoulder or overlap rounded joint caps on limb ends so no gaps or holes appear during rotation.
* Alpha Channels & Clean Edges: Ensure transparent areas use clean alpha channels rather than flattened backgrounds (Preparing and importing still images).
4. Cutout Rigs: Anchor Points & Parenting Hierarchy
Cutout animation relies on a digital paper-puppet structure established through spatial pivots and relational chains:


* Anchor Points First: Transformations (Rotation and Scale) occur around each layer's Anchor Point (Adjust layer properties in After Effects). Use the Pan Behind (Anchor Point) tool (Y) to place each limb's anchor point directly at its anatomical pivot (shoulder, elbow, wrist, antenna base) before setting keyframes (Adjust layer properties in After Effects).
* Parent & Child Hierarchy: Use the Parent & Link pick whip in the Timeline panel so child layers inherit Position, Scale, and Rotation from their parent while still animating independently—for example, Hand → Lower Arm → Upper Arm → Torso (Adjust layer properties in After Effects). Use Null Object layers when you need an invisible master controller for the entire rig (Adjust layer properties in After Effects).
5. Puppet Tools: Mesh Deformation, Rigid Pins, & Overlap Control
After Effects' native Puppet tools deform a single layer like a flexible triangle mesh. Preventing unwanted "whole-body waving" requires precise pin selection:


* Use Native Puppet Tools (No Python Scripts): Agents should apply the native Puppet tools directly in After Effects rather than writing external Python scripts to simulate mesh bending.
* Pin Types in the Advanced Puppet Tool (default engine in After Effects 15.1+, Animating with Puppet tools):
   * Puppet Position Pin Tool (Yellow): Places Deform pins that move part of the mesh and auto-keyframe Position when dragged. Use as few pins as possible so motion stays natural (Animating with Puppet tools).
   * Puppet Starch Pin Tool (Red): Stiffens parts of the mesh so they resist distortion. Place Starch pins on the torso or rigid body core to prevent the whole character from waving when a limb or antenna moves (Animating with Puppet tools). (Note: In the Legacy engine, Starch pins use Amount and Extent controls; in the Advanced engine, Starch pins stiffen the region between pins and can be converted to Position pins, while Mesh Rotation Refinement controls overall stiffness vs. flexibility.)
   * Puppet Overlap Pin Tool (Blue): Controls apparent depth (In Front value and Extent) when deformed parts cross over one another—for example, keeping a waving arm or antenna in front of the head (Animating with Puppet tools). Use Hold keyframes if animating In Front over time (Animating with Puppet tools).
   * Advanced Pins (Blue-Green) & Bend Pins (Orange-Brown): Advanced pins control Position, Scale, and Rotation; Bend pins automatically follow surrounding pins while letting you animate Scale and Rotation—ideal for wagging tails, breathing chests, or swaying appendages (Animating with Puppet tools).
6. Agent Demonstration Briefs (Local After Effects)
Demo A — Vector vs. Raster Scaling & Precomp Test
* Objective: Verify scaling limits and correct precomposition order for vector puppet meshes.
* Locked Setup: Import standard .psd and .ai assets via Retain Layer Sizes.
* Agent Parameters: Set scale to 300% on both layers. Toggle Continuously Rasterize on vector asset. Precompose vector layer prior to applying Puppet Position pins to avoid spatial transformation conflicts.
Demo B — Inprinted Cutout Rig (Pivot & Hierarchy)
* Objective: Construct a 3-part articulated arm with clean mechanical joints.
* Locked Setup: Separate artwork into Torso, Upper_Limb, and Lower_Limb with extended joint caps.
* Agent Parameters: Relocate anchor points to joint pivot centers using the Pan Behind tool (Y). Link hierarchy via pick whip: Lower_Limb → Upper_Limb → Torso. Animate Rotation keyframes across 60 frames.
Demo C — Whole-Body Distortion Fix (Position + Starch Pins)
* Objective: Eliminate body shear during extreme extremity movement.
* Locked Setup: Single flat character layer with flexible appendages.
* Agent Parameters: Apply Puppet Position Pin to hand/foot; translate pin to demonstrate unintended torso movement. Place Puppet Starch Pins along chest and spine to isolate distortion purely to the moving limb.
Demo D — Bee Antennae Follow-Through & Overlap (No Python)
* Objective: Demonstrate natural physics, drag, and depth layering natively.
* Locked Setup: Character head layer with twin antennae.
* Agent Parameters: Anchor head base with Starch pins. Apply Bend pins to antennae midpoints and Position pins to tips. Offset keyframes by 2-4 frames to simulate inertia/secondary movement. Apply Puppet Overlap pins to force designated antenna in front of head geometry during overlap phase.
